<meta type=article>
  <title | The Effect of Elephants on Climate>
  <subtitle | Evidence from Sub-Saharan Africa>
  <author | Jane Goodall>
  <author | David Attenborough>
  <abstract | This paper presents evidence that elephant populations significantly affect regional climate patterns through their role in shaping vegetation and carbon storage.>
</meta>

<section | Introduction>
Elephant populations have been **declining** for decades. Their role in shaping
ecosystems is *understated* in most climate models.

Three mechanisms are relevant: seed dispersal, vegetation management, and
trampling-mediated soil aeration.

<section | Methods>
We compiled census data from 12 national parks across sub-Saharan Africa.

<sub-section | Data sources>
The IUCN African Elephant Database provided population counts. Vegetation
cover came from MODIS satellite imagery.

<sub-section | Statistical approach>
We fit a hierarchical Bayesian model with park-level random effects.

<figure src="assets/elephant.jpg" | An adult African elephant in Tanzania, 2018.>

<section | Results>
We find a robust positive correlation between elephant density and woody-cover
turnover, controlling for rainfall and human disturbance.

<blockquote | The data are consistent with an ecosystem-engineering role for megaherbivores, as proposed by Owen-Smith (1988).>

<section | Discussion>
The implications for conservation policy are significant. Elephant
reintroduction may be a cost-effective climate intervention in degraded
savanna landscapes.

<aside | Note on terminology: we use "African elephant" throughout to mean *Loxodonta africana*, excluding the forest elephant *Loxodonta cyclotis*.>

Further work is needed to establish causation rather than correlation.

- Census data collection
- MODIS satellite imagery analysis
- Bayesian statistical modeling
